WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.043
A spike fires at one spot.

2
00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:08.463
How does it reach the far end, and why doesn't it come back?

3
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:13.627
Inward current at the active patch spreads along the core of the axon.

4
00:00:14.387 --> 00:00:16.607
It brings the next stretch of membrane to threshold.

5
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.970
That stretch fires, and its current spreads on to the next.

6
00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.630
The spike moves along, patch by patch.

7
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:25.773
Here is a question.

8
00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:28.933
Current spreads both ways from the active patch.

9
00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:31.273
So why doesn't the spike turn back?

10
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:37.637
The membrane just behind has just fired.

11
00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.397
Its sodium channels are inactivated.

12
00:00:41.377 --> 00:00:44.037
It is refractory, so it cannot be excited again.

13
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.810
The axon itself has no preferred direction.

14
00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.330
Stimulate the middle, and spikes travel both ways.

15
00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.390
A spike travels away from wherever it started.

16
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.200
Speed depends first on diameter.

17
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.920
A wider axon has lower internal resistance, so current spreads farther before

18
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:03.920
leaking away.

19
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.370
Think of a wide pipe against a narrow, leaky one.

20
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.190
Farther spread means a faster spike, which is why the squid giant axon is so wide.

21
00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:18.803
The other way to speed up is myelin, glial membrane wrapped around the axon.

22
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.433
Picture a leaky garden hose wrapped in tape.

23
00:01:23.073 --> 00:01:24.093
Less current leaks out.

24
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:27.103
Myelin also lowers capacitance.

25
00:01:28.023 --> 00:01:31.663
Think of a narrow water tank: the same pour raises its level more.

26
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:36.433
With less capacitance, the same charge raises the voltage more.

27
00:01:37.313 --> 00:01:39.313
The voltage climbs as the charge arrives.

28
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:43.727
So current from one gap reaches the next fast and strong.

29
00:01:44.607 --> 00:01:48.527
The gaps are nodes of Ranvier, where sodium channels regenerate the spike.

30
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.690
This is saltatory conduction, from the Latin for leap.

31
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.190
Excitation skips the wrapped stretch.

32
00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.890
Current does not. It flows through the axon from node to node.

33
00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:02.233
Speeds vary widely.

34
00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:07.633
In mammalian nerve, unmyelinated C fibres conduct at about walking pace.

35
00:02:08.513 --> 00:02:12.253
The largest myelinated fibres are roughly a hundred times faster.

36
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.287
Now strip the myelin from one stretch.

37
00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:17.107
What happens to the spike?

38
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.440
The bare stretch leaks current and has more membrane to charge.

39
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.620
The next node is reached late, or not at all.

40
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.700
Conduction slows or blocks.

41
00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:32.233
It does not always block.

42
00:02:33.073 --> 00:02:37.573
In some fibres the bare stretch develops sodium and potassium currents of its own.

43
00:02:38.433 --> 00:02:40.293
Then it conducts slowly and continuously.

44
00:02:41.417 --> 00:02:42.497
Back to the puzzles.

45
00:02:43.537 --> 00:02:46.817
Overshoot: sodium briefly pulls the balance point above zero.

46
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.317
Threshold: sodium feedback sustains itself.

47
00:02:51.277 --> 00:02:54.917
All-or-none: brief pushes from rest give spikes of about the same size.

48
00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:00.310
Refractoriness: inactivated sodium channels need time to recover.

49
00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.850
Travel: current spreads ahead, and refractory membrane behind blocks the way back.

50
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.700
Spread ahead, refractory behind.

51
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.420
Wider and myelinated means faster.
